-- title:
--   Counterexample 3.5 — for every $d \ge 2$, a $C^d$ instance in $\mathbb R^2$ where every line-search Frank-Wolfe sequence has $f(x_t) \to \min_C f$ but does not converge
-- statement:
--   For every integer $d\ge2$ there exist a nonempty compact convex $\mathcal C\subset\mathbb R^2$, an open convex $\mathcal D\supset\mathcal C$, a convex function $f:\mathcal D\to\mathbb R$ that is $d$-times continuously differentiable on $\mathcal D$ and has $L$-Lipschitz-continuous gradient on $\mathcal D$ for some $L>0$ (so $\mathcal C$ and $f$ satisfy Assumption 2.3), and a start point $x_0\in\mathcal C$ such that
--
--   1. a sequence generated by the Frank-Wolfe algorithm with the line-search strategy (iii) from $x_0$ exists, and
--   2. every such sequence $(x_t)$, for every choice of the oracle outputs $v_t$ and of the line-search minimizers $\gamma_t$, satisfies
--   $$
--   f(x_t)\xrightarrow[t\to\infty]{}\min_{\mathcal C}f\qquad\text{and}\qquad (x_t)_{t\in\mathbb N}\text{ does not converge.}
--   $$
--
--   The paper's point is that the iterates of Frank-Wolfe may fail to converge even when the linear minimization oracle is specified and the objective is smooth and convex: the non-convergence is intrinsic to the algorithm, not an artifact of adversarial tie-breaking.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--
--   - generalization: C and f satisfying Assumption 2.3 → additionally f is d-times continuously differentiable on D, for every given d ≥ 2; reason: the abstract (p.1) and Contributions (p.2) claim it and the construction via Theorem 3.1 delivers it; dropping it gives the printed statement
--   - generalization: any generated sequence does not converge → additionally every generated sequence has f(x_t) → min_C f; reason: claimed in the Contributions paragraph (p.2) and given by Theorem 2.4; dropping it gives the printed statement
--   - generalization: any sequence generated … does not converge → additionally a generated sequence from x₀ exists; reason: rules out a vacuous "every sequence"; the paper exhibits the sequence x_t = C_t or B_t
--   - encoding: "any sequence generated by Frank-Wolfe using (iii)" → every x with IsFWRun C f (lineSearch f) x₀ x; reason: Remark 2.2(i): every oracle selection and every line-search minimizer
--   - encoding: min_C f; does not converge → IsLeast (f '' C) m; ¬ ∃ z, Tendsto x atTop (𝓝 z) in ℝ²; reason: the same notions
--   - encoding: f : D → ℝ → f : ℝ² → ℝ with every property (convexity, smoothness, sublevel sets, gradient, Hessian) required on D only; reason: the values of f outside D never enter; D is open, so gradients and Hessians at points of D are the true ones
-- source:
--   Bolte, Combettes & Pauwels, The Iterates of the Frank-Wolfe Algorithm May Not Converge, arXiv:2202.08711v1, Counterexample 3.5, p.5 (with the claims of the abstract, p.1, and of the Contributions paragraph, p.2)

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_FWIterates_Assumption23
import Definitions.Def_FWIterates_IsFWRun

open scoped RealInnerProductSpace
open Filter Topology

namespace FWIterates

/-- **Counterexample 3.5** (arXiv:2202.08711v1, Counterexample 3.5, p.5), with the clauses of the
abstract and the Contributions paragraph (pp.1–2). For every `d ≥ 2` there exist `C` and `f`
satisfying Assumption 2.3 in `ℝ²`, with `f` `d`-times continuously differentiable on `D`, and a
start point `x₀ ∈ C` such that a sequence generated by the Frank-Wolfe algorithm with the
line-search strategy (iii) from `x₀` exists, and every such sequence satisfies `f(xₜ) → min_C f`
and does not converge.

**Formalization Note** "Generated" is Remark 2.2(i) (`IsFWRun`): every oracle selection
`vₜ ∈ argmin_{v ∈ C} ⟨v, ∇f(xₜ)⟩` and every line-search minimizer `γₜ` is covered. `min_C f` is
the `m` with `IsLeast (f '' C) m`. Non-convergence is of the sequence in `ℝ²`. The existence of a
run rules out a vacuous "every run" clause. -/
theorem counterexample_3_5 (d : ℕ) (hd : 2 ≤ d) :
    ∃ (C D : Set (EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin 2))) (f : EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin 2) → ℝ) (L : ℝ)
      (x₀ : EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin 2)),
      Assumption23 C D f L ∧ ContDiffOn ℝ d f D ∧ x₀ ∈ C ∧
      (∃ x : ℕ → EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin 2), IsFWRun C f (lineSearch f) x₀ x) ∧
      ∃ m : ℝ, IsLeast (f '' C) m ∧
        ∀ x : ℕ → EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin 2), IsFWRun C f (lineSearch f) x₀ x →
          Tendsto (fun t => f (x t)) atTop (𝓝 m) ∧
            ¬ ∃ z : EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin 2), Tendsto x atTop (𝓝 z) := by sorry

end FWIterates
